  "headline": "Markets price a hot, inflationary US economy, not a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "The configuration is an inflation story with growth still intact. Brent is up 75% year-to-date and 26% over three months, the 10-year yield sits at 5.22%, up 105 basis points this year and in the top decile of its range, and the dollar index is at a 52-week high after a 3% monthly gain. Gold, meanwhile, is near the bottom of its range, down 4.7% on the month: there is no flight to safety here, only high real yields and a strong dollar weighing on a dollar-priced asset. Copper near the top of its range, up 20% year-to-date and rising 3% through a stronger dollar this month, says cyclical demand is holding.",
    "The curve has bear-flattened, the 30-year–10-year gap narrowing 3 basis points over the month as both ends rose, which reads as the market pricing tighter policy against an energy-fed price shock rather than a lasting growth premium. Bitcoin outrunning gold by 13% on the month with calm volatility speaks to liquidity and risk appetite, not fear. The tension is oil at $107 against growth; copper and the copper/gold ratio, up 8% on the month, carry more weight, so this is an economy absorbing a cost shock, not buckling under it.",
    "Two outliers: USD/CAD at a 52-week high despite triple-digit oil shows dollar demand overriding the usual commodity-currency support, and USD/INR fell 1.6% today against the grain. This week's 5–8 basis point dip in yields with a flat dollar is consolidation, not a change of regime."
  ],
