      "According to Drop Site News, Ansarallah has threatened to strike Aramco facilities and Iran is tightening control over the Strait of Hormuz, while Zelenskyy is quoted as saying Russia violated an energy ceasefire. The channel is Gulf supply and shipping risk, and the calls follow directly: bullish for Brent, though with crude up 5% this week and 75% this year a large part of that risk premium is already in the price, and supportive of the dollar as the funding currency of choice in a supply shock. It is a headwind for Treasuries at the long end, since an energy-driven inflation impulse pushes the 10-year and 30-year yields higher, and a headwind for the rupee given India's import bill, today's 1.6% rebound notwithstanding. Gold's bid is the weak link: it has not responded so far and is unlikely to lead while real yields stay this high."
